{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"call_fd03637e16414f9c9290935d","type":"tool_result","content":"{\"body\":\"## Symptom\\n\\n`scaffold.runtime` and `scaffold.runtime.sqlite` both fail at the **final** gate,\\n`runtime.health.listener-unreachable`, identically:\\n\\n```\\ngarnet healthReports.test_only_garnet_resp missed its 30s transition;\\nlast=Unhealthy: RESP listener unhealthy: ECONNREFUSED at localhost:18999 after 1 ms; received=\\\"\\\"\\n```\\n\\nObserved in both tiers of run\\n[`33541672224`](https://github.com/rickylabs/netscript/actions/runs/33541672224). Everything before it\\npasses — `readiness-fixture`, `aspire-start`, `wait.postgres`, `wait.garnet`, workers, sagas,\\ntriggers, auth, streams, app, describe.\\n\\n`ECONNREFUSED` after **1 ms** is a refused TCP connect: nothing is listening on 18999. The D-101\\nsynthetic listener is owned by the Aspire-managed `listener-fault-controller` task, and that task\\nnever starts.\\n\\n## Root cause — proven statically, no runtime needed\\n\\n`injectReadinessFixtureApps` (`prepare-readiness-fixture.ts`) generates its two fixture apps by\\ncalling `generateRegisterApps` **in isolation**, then splices the emitted blocks into the project's\\nown `register-apps.mts`. Since #1837 the apps generator names identifiers **positionally**\\n(`app_${appIndex}`) instead of from the resource name, and the isolated fixture generation starts at\\nindex 0 — so it emits identifiers the host module has already declared.\\n\\nReproduced by running the real generator plus the real injector against a realistic one-app project:\\n\\n```\\n--- REAL project declares:\\n    const app_0 = builder.addExecutable(\\\"app\\\", 'deno', app_0_workdir, ['task', \\\"start\\\"]);\\n    apps.set(\\\"app\\\", app_0);\\n\\n--- AFTER fixture injection:\\n    const app_0 = builder.addExecutable(\\\"app\\\", ...);                    \\u003c-- project\\n    const app_0 = builder.addExecutable(\\\"readiness-dead-port\\\", ...);    \\u003c-- COLLISION\\n    const app_1 = builder.addExecutable(\\\"listener-fault-controller\\\", ...);\\n\\n--- duplicate const declarations: [ \\\"const app_0\\\" ]\\n```\\n\\nA duplicate `const` is a **SyntaxError** in the emitted `.mts`. The fixture apps never register, the\\ncontroller never binds 18999, and the RESP check on the synthetic listener is refused for the whole\\nwindow.\\n\\nNote the injected blocks also *reference* `app_0_workdir` / `app_1_workdir`, which the sliced block\\nmay not carry — so identifier renaming must cover the whole block, not just the `addExecutable` line.\\n\\nBefore #1837 the generator used `safeIdentifier(name)`, producing `readiness_dead_port` and\\n`listener_fault_controller` — unique by construction, no collision possible.\\n\\n## Why it surfaced only now, and what it is *not*\\n\\nIt is **not** caused by #1858 (`fix/garnet-readiness-timeout`), on whose run it was found. That PR\\ndoes not touch `prepare-readiness-fixture.ts`, `listener-unreachable-fixture.ts`, or the apps\\ngenerator, and the check it actually changed — `runtime.wait.garnet` — **passed in ~2 s**. Its\\nrewritten RESP probe reports the closed port quickly and accurately; the old probe would have walled\\nfor 300 s on the same condition.\\n\\nIt is also **not** the 30 s Garnet timeout risk: the 30 s here is `REPORT_DEADLINE_MS` in\\n`listener-unreachable-fixture.ts`, pre-existing and untouched, and `listenerFaultExpectations` still\\nuses 300 s.\\n\\n`runtime.health.listener-unreachable` is the **last** gate in the suite. It has been unreachable since\\n#1837 because the suite stopped earlier — at `runtime.flow-b-fixture` (#1863), then\\n`runtime.readiness-fixture` (#1870), then `runtime.wait.workers` (#1877). #1865 cleared all three, so\\nthis is simply the next one revealed.\\n\\n## The pattern — fourth consumer break from one change\\n\\n| Issue | Producer change | Stale consumer |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| #1863 | #1837 renamed the per-plugin block comment to an ordinal | `prepare-flow-b-fixture.ts` literal |\\n| #1870 | #1837 replaced a duplicate-tolerant check with a uniqueness requirement | `Mode: 'Auto'` emits two attachments |\\n| #1877 | #1864 renamed the worker startup log line | `wait-for-workers-runtime.ts` markers |\\n| **this** | **#1837 renamed app identifiers to positional** | fixture apps generated in isolation collide with the host's |\\n\\nThe first three coupled on **strings**; this one couples on **generated identifier namespace**, which\\nis worse — it produces a module that does not parse rather than a check that does not match.\\n\\n## Bounded fix\\n\\nMake the injected fixture blocks use identifiers that cannot collide with the host module. Either\\nrewrite the sliced block's `app_\\u003cn\\u003e` identifiers (including `_workdir`/`_otel` suffixed ones) to a\\nfixture-specific namespace, or generate the fixture apps at an index offset past the host's app count.\\nRenaming is preferable: an offset silently breaks again if the host's app count changes between\\ngeneration and injection.\\n\\nOut of scope: do not change the apps generator's positional naming — it is behaving as #1837 intends,\\nand other consumers now depend on it.\\n\\n## Definition of Done\\n\\n- [ ] Injecting the readiness fixture into a project that already declares `app_0` produces no duplicate `const` declaration, proven by a test over **real generator output** rather than a hand-written string.\\n- [ ] Every identifier in the injected block is renamed consistently, including `_workdir` and any other suffixed references.\\n- [ ] A test proves the emitted module parses after injection (e.g. type-check or parse the result), not merely that the text differs.\\n- [ ] `listener-fault-controller` starts and binds 18999, and `runtime.health.listener-unreachable` passes in both hosted tiers.\\n- [ ] No change to `generate-register-apps.ts`; `deno.lock` unchanged.\\n\",\"state\":\"open\",\"title\":\"fix(e2e): readiness-fixture app injection collides with the project's positional app identifiers, so listener-fault-controller never starts\"}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"cdb5a58b-f1c0-4dee-9493-e77a27dbfa08","uuid":"1c9496b3-845b-42c6-a0cf-9b57334b5c7d","timestamp":"2026-09-01T20:19:28.321Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"{\"body\":\"## Symptom\\n\\n`scaffold.runtime` and `scaffold.runtime.sqlite` both fail at the **final** gate,\\n`runtime.health.listener-unreachable`, identically:\\n\\n```\\ngarnet healthReports.test_only_garnet_resp missed its 30s transition;\\nlast=Unhealthy: RESP listener unhealthy: ECONNREFUSED at localhost:18999 after 1 ms; received=\\\"\\\"\\n```\\n\\nObserved in both tiers of run\\n[`33541672224`](https://github.com/rickylabs/netscript/actions/runs/33541672224). Everything before it\\npasses — `readiness-fixture`, `aspire-start`, `wait.postgres`, `wait.garnet`, workers, sagas,\\ntriggers, auth, streams, app, describe.\\n\\n`ECONNREFUSED` after **1 ms** is a refused TCP connect: nothing is listening on 18999. The D-101\\nsynthetic listener is owned by the Aspire-managed `listener-fault-controller` task, and that task\\nnever starts.\\n\\n## Root cause — proven statically, no runtime needed\\n\\n`injectReadinessFixtureApps` (`prepare-readiness-fixture.ts`) generates its two fixture apps by\\ncalling `generateRegisterApps` **in isolation**, then splices the emitted blocks into the project's\\nown `register-apps.mts`. Since #1837 the apps generator names identifiers **positionally**\\n(`app_${appIndex}`) instead of from the resource name, and the isolated fixture generation starts at\\nindex 0 — so it emits identifiers the host module has already declared.\\n\\nReproduced by running the real generator plus the real injector against a realistic one-app project:\\n\\n```\\n--- REAL project declares:\\n    const app_0 = builder.addExecutable(\\\"app\\\", 'deno', app_0_workdir, ['task', \\\"start\\\"]);\\n    apps.set(\\\"app\\\", app_0);\\n