jected INSIDE the host's own if-block? No — injection point is before `return apps;`.\n\nHmm, let me look at what actually happens in the scaffold.runtime E2E: the suite \"type-checks the generated workspaces\". If that type-check is `deno check` on the whole workspace, sibling-scope duplicates pass...\n\nWait, wait. Let me re-read the pre-fix appBlock slicing more carefully. Maybe the slice for readiness-dead-port DOESN'T include the `if (...) {` line? blockStart = lastIndexOf('  // --- app ', registrationIndex) — registrationIndex is at `apps.set(\"readiness-dead-port\", app_0)` — the lastIndexOf finds the comment of app 0's block in the FIXTURE generation. So the slice starts at the comment. Includes everything. Yes.\n\nHmm — so was the runtime failure caused by something else entirely and the issue's diagnosis is just WRONG? But the RED test proves the collision textually exists... and the FIX renames identifiers... but if the pre-fix module worked at runtime, the fix wouldn't repair the runtime failure!\n\nHold on. Let me reconsider. Is the real scaffolded project's register-apps.mts really generated by generateRegisterApps? Or is it modified by other steps — e.g., other fixture injectors (flow-b, workers, etc.) that also splice blocks in? In scaffold.runtime, several gates inject fixtures: prepare-flow-b-fixture, prepare-readiness-fixture, wait-for-workers, etc. Maybe a DIFFERENT injector also injects app blocks (e.g., flow-b fixture) at the same marker, in a way that creates SAME-SCOPE collisions with the readiness fixture's app_0?\n\nIf flow-b fixture injects raw generator output with `const app_0` at the top level... Let me check prepare-flow-b-fixture.ts — it was fixed in #1863 for the comment rename. Does it inject app blocks? Its name suggests flow-b. Let me look at what the real register-apps.mts looks like after ALL injections in scaffold.runtime.\n\nActually — wait. Much simpler: what does the REAL host project look like? The scaffold.runtime suite scaffolds a project with plugins; its apps config... The issue says the REAL project declares `const app_0 = builder.addExecutable(\"app\", 'deno', app_0_workdir, ['task', \"start\"]);` — one app named \"app\".\n\nHmm OK, but here's the thing — maybe the actual runtime failure is NOT the emitted module failing to parse, but the readiness fixture's `deno check`-equivalent step in the suite: \"type-checks the generated workspaces\" — the suite runs a type-check AFTER fixture injection. If the type-check used `tsc` or deno check on the whole workspace, sibling-scope dupes pass.\n\nSo mechanically, with the pre-fix code, the fixture apps SHOULD register... Let me look for a different explanation of the ECONNREFUSED. Maybe the OTEL loop: `for (const [key, value] of Object.entries(app_0_otel)) { await app_0.withEnvironment(key, value); }` — inside the FIXTURE's if-block, `app_0` is the fixture's const. Fine.\n\nHmm hm hm. But wait — is it possible the REAL host register-apps.mts does NOT wrap app blocks in `if` blocks? What if the real project was scaffolded in a mode where... no, the generator always wraps.\n\nLet me look at the ACTUAL evidence more carefully — is there a real failing CI log? The issue links run 33541672224. I can't fetch logs easily (gh api runs logs endpoint needs auth and is big). Hmm.\n\nActually — hold on. Let me re-read the issue's repro output:\n\n```\n--- AFTER fixture injection:\n    const app_0 = builder.addExecutable(\"app\", ...);                    <-- project\n    const app_0 = builder.addExecutable(\"readiness-dead-port\", ...);    <-- COLLISION\n    const app_1 = builder.addExecutable(\"listener-fault-controller\", ...);\n```\n\nThe repro shows the app_0 declarations at the SAME indentation (4 spaces) — inside their respective if-blocks. Fine.\n\n\"Note the injected blocks also *reference* `app_0_workdir` / `app_1_workdir`, which the sliced block may not carry\" — hmm, \"which the sliced block may not carry\"? The sliced block DOES carry the const declarations. Unless the slice boundary cuts them off? blockStart = lastIndexOf('  // --- app ', registrationIndex). Registration is apps.set — near the END of the block. lastIndexOf finds the START of the same block. Includes everything.\n\nOK here's another thought — maybe the REAL failing mechanism is that the fixture apps' if-blocks never run because... `config.Apps[\"readiness-dead-port\"]` — undefined → `?.Enabled` → undefined → `undefined !== false` → TRUE. Runs.\n\nHonestly — the runtime mechanism question may be unresolvable statically here, and my job is the evaluator questions F1-F7, not re-diagnosing the runtime. BUT the brief says: \"Try to break the claims; a PASS you cannot defend is worse than a FAIL.\" The claim structure:\n\n- F4: \"Does the emitted module parse? This is the load-bearing check.\" — For the POST-fix module: yes (proven). \n- The issue's root-cause narrative claims the pre-fix module was a SyntaxError. My experiment shows the pre-fix module PASSES deno check at least for a one-app host with my stubs. So the narrative's mechanism is overstated/wrong.\n\nDoes that change the verdict? The defect defined by the RED test is text-level duplicate identifier declarations — real, proven at RED. The fix eliminates duplicates and dangling references, proven by my probes. The runtime binding of 18999 is explicitly out of scope (\"runtime proof comes later\").\n\nBUT — if the pre-fix module parses fine, does the fix actually FIX the runtime failure? If the pre-fix module was never a SyntaxError, then the fixture apps should have registered pre-fix, and 18999 should have bound... so the runtime defect must have a different cause, and this PR might NOT fix the runtime symptom! That's a serious question for the verdict — potentially a blocking finding: \"the fix may not address the actual runtime failure.\"\n\nWait — before concluding, let me dig more. Maybe the real host register-apps.mts differs: in the real scaffold.runtime project, OTHER