controller binds 18999... The gate failed with ECONNREFUSED after 1ms — nothing listening.\n\nOK — maybe the actual cause IS discoverable: the issue says \"the D-101 synthetic listener is owned by the Aspire-managed listener-fault-controller task, and that task never starts.\" Aspire starts the task... the fixture app registered as `readiness-dead-port`... hmm wait — pre-fix, apps.set(\"listener-fault-controller\", app_1) — inside the fixture if-block — registers fine.\n\nHmm, unless the Aspire AppHost type-checks the helpers at build (the C# side compiles the .mts?) — no.\n\nWAIT. Maybe I should check the REAL sequence in scaffold.runtime: the suite runs `scaffold` → `generate` → ... → `aspire-start` → health checks. The issue says everything before `runtime.health.listener-unreachable` passes, including `runtime.readiness-fixture` (which runs prepareReadinessFixture). If prepareReadinessFixture succeeded and the module parsed at runtime (deno check gate is BEFORE aspire-start? or not present in suite?)...\n\nThe suite description: \"generates plugin registries, type-checks the generated workspaces, restores and starts Aspire\". So type-check happens before aspire-start. Pre-fix: type-check of generated workspaces — would it have FAILED? If the type-check includes aspire/.helpers/register-apps.mts — sibling-scope duplicates are legal — passes. Hmm.\n\nBUT WAIT — what if the type-check DOESN'T pass because of the `if (config.Apps[...]?.Enabled !== false)` DUPLICATE... no.\n\nOK, alternative hypothesis for the runtime failure that the fix does address... hmm, what if pre-fix the fixture's readiness-dead-port app would SHADOW... no.\n\nHonestly, maybe the runtime failure had a completely different cause and the \"static diagnosis\" was confirmation-biased. But my job: the PR's claim is that the collision is real (proven by RED) and the fix eliminates it (proven by my probes). The runtime symptom resolution is explicitly deferred (\"runtime proof comes later, when integrated into #1858 and #1885\"). The brief's F4 says the load-bearing check is that the emitted module parses — for the POST-fix module it does; and importantly, the brief warns: \"If the committed test only counts strings, say so: that would make the suite vacuous against exactly the failure mode in F3.\" The committed test does include a compile check — good.\n\nHowever — the mechanism overstatement deserves a finding: the issue's claim \"A duplicate const is a SyntaxError in the emitted .mts\" is REFUTED for the shapes I tested (host/fixture blocks are sibling scopes → legal). The pre-fix emitted module passes `deno check`. Therefore the PR's causal story for the runtime ECONNREFUSED is unproven — and possibly wrong. The PR itself (body) says \"The regression uses real generator output and requires the injected module to type-check, so partial suffix renames cannot pass\" — that part is accurate. The PR doesn't itself claim the SyntaxError mechanism; the ISSUE does.\n\nHmm — hold on. Let me think once more about whether there's a shape where the duplicate IS a SyntaxError. The fixture if-block conditions: `config.Apps[\"readiness-dead-port\"]?.Enabled !== false`. What if... the HOST's register-apps.mts in the REAL project contains additional top-level `const app_0` — e.g., the workers plugin injects background processors via register-background.mts (different file). Plugins registries — different files.\n\nWhat about the possibility that the REAL host project's apps config included an app that generates blocks NOT wrapped in if? No — always wrapped.\n\nActually — WAIT. One more: what about VITE-type app (`Type: 'app'`) with ServiceReferences — inside the block: `{ const vite = ...; const endpoint = ...; }` — inner scopes. Fine.\n\nWhat about the fixture block being injected when the host has ZERO apps? Template: `// No apps configured` comment instead of blocks — injection still before return. Legal.\n\nSo: for every shape, pre-fix module is legal JS. The SyntaxError claim is wrong. UNLESS deno check isn't the relevant consumer: the D-101 listener-unreachable gate... the emitted register-apps.mts is executed by Aspire's AppHost via a Deno/JS runtime — `Jupyter`... The Aspire SDK imports the .mts — executing a module with duplicate sibling-scope consts is fine.\n\nTherefore: the runtime mechanism in the issue is likely WRONG, and the PR may not fix the runtime failure. BUT the brief anticipates exactly this class of concern: \"Absence of a runtime receipt is not a FAIL reason — say so explicitly. The runtime proof comes later.\" And F1-F7 are the questions that decide the verdict — all answerable as CONFIRMED. The mechanism overstatement is an issue-authoring concern, not a code defect. The fix makes the fixture namespace disjoint — which is a genuine robustness improvement and matches the issue's DoD (which is what the PR closes).\n\nHmm, but hmm. If the fix doesn't fix the runtime failure, then when integrated into #1858/#1885, the gate will still fail... I should flag this as a risk / non-blocking observation with my evidence: pre-fix module passes deno check; sibling scopes legal; the actual runtime mechanism for \"task never starts\" remains unproven. Actually — wait, let me dig ONE more level: maybe Aspire's AppHost does fail to start the helper due to... the AppHost (C#) runs the register-apps.mts through a Node/Deno-compatible JS engine (the Aspire preview SDK for Deno?). If it's a JS engine that treats the file as a MODULE with top-level... still legal.\n\nAlternatively — maybe the REAL host register-apps.mts at runtime was NOT generated by the same template I read! The scaffold.runtime project's register-apps.mts could be MODIFIED by the e2e suite before the readiness fixture: look at what `prepare-flow-b-fixture.ts` does — maybe it also splices app blocks into register-apps.mts (flow-b needs an app?). If flow-b's injector ALSO injected `const app_0` at a position where it collides same-scope... Let me actually read