{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"gen-1788295149-597ZcWtvRs8OBqnvOhHu","type":"message","role":"assistant","container":null,"content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Only the readiness fixture touches register-apps.mts in the e2e source. Let me also check the suites dir and any other places writing register-apps (e.g., `suites/`).\n\nAlso — let me reconsider. Is there maybe a REAL runtime parse consumer that is stricter? The Aspire AppHost runs the helper through... let me check how aspire runs these .mts helpers — \"aspire/.helpers\" executed by the AppHost via Deno? If the C# AppHost spawns `deno run` on the module, sibling-scope duplicates are still legal.\n\nHmm wait, actually — hold on. Let me recheck my pre-fix probe. Did the injected output ACTUALLY contain both fixture blocks with duplicates? The probe printed dups = [\"app_0_workdir\",\"app_0\",\"app_0_otel\"] — yes, from the RED injector. And deno check exit 0. Solid.\n\nBut — one more consideration: `deno check` type-checks. Did it actually type-check the module, or skip? Output \"Check file:///...register-apps.mts\" — yes it checked.\n\nSo statically: pre-fix module is VALID TypeScript. The runtime symptom (listener task never started) therefore had some other cause — OR the collision analysis in the issue is right about the mechanism but my host shape differs from the real one in a way that matters. What differs between my probe host and the REAL scaffold.runtime host?\n\nThe REAL project is scaffolded with plugins (workers, sagas, triggers, streams). Its register-apps.mts comes from the real generator with the project's apps. The issue's repro shows one app named \"app\" (task). My probe used exactly that. So same shape.\n\nThen at runtime with pre-fix code:\n1. readiness fixture injects two blocks with duplicate text-level consts — legal module.\n2. Aspire starts; registerApps runs; fixture apps register; listener-fault-controller task starts; binds 18999.\n\nUnless the runtime AppHost executes register-apps.mts with a REAL aspire.mts module whose `addExecutable` etc. behave differently... irrelevant to parse.\n\nHmm, but WAIT — what if the real host has MULTIPLE apps? Then pre-fix: fixture app_0 vs host app_0 — sibling scopes, legal. Still fine.\n\nSo statically the pre-fix module should have worked, yet CI observed ECONNREFUSED across two tiers. Something must actually break at runtime. What could make the controller task not start...\n\nOH WAIT. I think I finally see it. Look at the fixture injection for `readiness-dead-port` — its `Workdir: '.netscript/e2e/readiness-dead-port'`. And `listener-fault-controller` — `Workdir: LISTENER_FAULT_CONTROLLER_DIR`. Aspire `addExecutable(name, 'deno', workdir, ['task', 'start'])`. The AppHost runs `deno task start` in each workdir. For the controller: `deno run --allow-net --allow-read --allow-write main.ts` — binds 18999 or 18998 depending on state file...\n\nThe state file: LISTENER_FAULT_STATE_FILE written by writeControllerFixture with `{ revision: 0, postgresOpen: true, garnetOpen: true }`. The controller binds a listener... The gate `runtime.health.listener-unreachable` expects the garnet RESP listener on 18999 to be UNHEALTHY (fault injection: the controller CLOSES/never opens 18999 → gate expects listener-unreachable report). The health report says \"RESP listener unhealthy: ECONNREFUSED at localhost:18999\" — that's the EXPECTED fault! The gate then checks the health report transiitons to Unhealthy within 30s (\"missed its 30s transition\"). The failure message: \"garnet healthReports.test_only_garnet_resp missed its 30s transition; last=Unhealthy: RESP listener unhealthy: ECONNREFUSED...\" — hmm, \"last=Unhealthy\" — the transition TARGET... The gate expects a transition (maybe Unhealthy→something?) — \"listener-unreachable\" gate name suggests it VERIFIES that a dead listener is REPORTED as Unhealthy. last=Unhealthy... missed its transition — maybe it expected Healthy→Unhealthy→(ack?) or the health check to