gisters the right resource. Fine.\n\nFINAL ANSWER on mechanism: statically refuted; runtime mechanism unproven by the PR's evidence; runtime proof explicitly deferred by the brief. I'll record this as a non-blocking observation (root-cause narrative overstated) — because the evaluator questions F1–F7 all resolve, and the fix is real, safe, and ceiling-compliant. Actually — should this be BLOCKING? The PR claims \"Closes #1898\" — the issue's acceptance requires the runtime gate to pass in hosted tiers (\"listener-fault-controller starts and binds 18999, and runtime.health.listener-unreachable passes in both hosted tiers\"). The PR body explicitly leaves that to the supervisor/integration. The brief says absence of runtime receipt is NOT a FAIL reason. So: PASS_IMPL with an explicit caveat that (a) runtime proof is deferred and (b) my static experiment contradicts the issue's SyntaxError narrative, so the deferred runtime proof is not just formal — it's genuinely unverified. Hmm — but is that honest as a PASS? The brief's F-questions define the verdict. F1 CONFIRMED, F2 CONFIRMED, F3 CONFIRMED, F4 CONFIRMED (post-fix parses; committed test not vacuous), F5 CONFIRMED, F6 CONFIRMED, F7 CONFIRMED. All seven → PASS_IMPL. The mechanism finding is a non-blocking observation but an important one.\n\nWait — one more check on F4 though: \"Establish that the injected output is syntactically valid — not merely that the text changed.\" Done for post-fix. And the committed test: contains deno check — NOT vacuous. But note: at RED, the compile check was UNREACHABLE (assertion before it fails first). That's fine — RED proves the collision; the compile check guards the fix.\n\nHmm, actually — one more F4 subtlety: does the committed test's compile check actually exercise the FIXTURE-injected module? Yes — it runs deno check on the injected source. Good.\n\nNow, before the verdict, also double-check the \"commit list + per-slice PR comments\" trail (evaluator protocol): PR #1899 body